page('/', 'Open tools for understanding wildlife', 'OpenXeroth shares Xeroth’s open tools and research using optics, acoustics and AI. Explore NatureCam, Djuma birds and acoustic re-identification research.', f'''
<section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">OpenXeroth</div><h1>Open tools for<br><em>understanding wildlife.</em></h1><p class="lead">We use cameras, sound and AI to study wildlife—and share the tools and evidence behind the work.</p><p>OpenXeroth is part of <a href="https://xeroth.ai">Xeroth</a>, whose mission is to advance AI to protect lives and landscapes. This is where our open conservation projects and research can be explored, used and questioned.</p></div><figure class="sensing-visual"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="sensing-title"><title id="sensing-title">Optics and acoustics contribute to a shared wildlife record</title><rect x="1" y="1" width="478" height="328" rx="24" fill="#eaf0e7"/><g fill="none" stroke="#365f47" stroke-width="3"><rect x="54" y="56" width="104" height="72" rx="12"/><circle cx="106" cy="92" r="23"/><circle cx="106" cy="92" r="10"/><path d="M168 93H230L280 165M55 231h20l8-26 13 52 12-75 13 94 12-70 12 47 12-22h28M190 231h40l50-66"/><rect x="282" y="113" width="143" height="104" rx="12"/><path d="M304 145h95m-95 24h65m-65 24h82"/></g><g fill="#20382d" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="18"><text x="65" y="160">Optics</text><text x="62" y="310">Acoustics</text><text x="287" y="253">Shared evidence</text></g></svg><figcaption>Two ways to observe. One record to investigate.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="section intro-projects"><div class="eyebrow">Explore OpenXeroth</div><h2>Choose where to begin.</h2><div class="grid3"><article class="card"><span class="number">01 / NatureCam</span><h3>Cameras, people and AI</h3><p>Meet the project, watch Djuma Cam, and learn about Hosana and AI assistance for human zoomies.</p><a class="link" href="/naturecam/">Explore NatureCam ↗</a></article><article class="card"><span class="number">02 / Djuma birds</span><h3>The day in birdsong</h3><p>Browse daily species suggestions and listen to calls analysed with BirdNET-Cloud.</p><a class="link" href="/djuma-birds/">Explore Djuma birds ↗</a></article><article class="card"><span class="number">03 / Research</span><h3>Testing whether AI can recognise individual animals</h3><p>Read our work on how recording conditions affect AI’s ability to recognise individual animals.</p><a class="link" href="/acoustic-re-id-research/">Explore the research ↗</a></article></div><p class="open-hardware">Also open: our <a href="https://github.com/OpenXeroth/xeroth-base-station">GNSS base-station reference implementation</a> supports precise positioning for field work.</p></section>''')
page('/naturecam/', 'NatureCam: wildlife observation at Djuma', 'Watch Djuma Cam and explore NatureCam, Hosana animal re-identification and AI assistance for volunteer camera operators.', f'''
<section class="hero"><div><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">OpenXeroth</a> / NatureCam</div><div class="eyebrow">The NatureCam project</div><h1>A wildlife camera.<br><em>A shared record of life.</em></h1><p class="lead">At Djuma’s Gowrie Dam, a remotely operated camera lets people around the world observe animals in their own habitat. NatureCam brings those observations together with optics, acoustics and AI.</p><p>Starting at Djuma, we’re developing tools to recognise returning animals and help the volunteer zoomies who follow the action.</p></div>{scene}</section>
<section class="section" id="watch"><div class="eyebrow">Gowrie Dam · Sabi Sands, South Africa</div><h2>Watch Djuma Cam.</h2><div class="live-frame" id="live-frame"><div id="djuma-player"></div><div class="stream-cover" id="stream-cover"><p id="stream-message" role="status">Watch the current broadcast from Djuma’s official YouTube channel.</p><button class="button" id="load-stream">Load the live stream ▶</button></div></div><p class="fine" id="stream-status" role="status">The player loads from YouTube when you choose to watch.</p><p>If the camera is offline or the player cannot connect, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@djumacam/streams">check Djuma’s latest broadcasts on YouTube</a>. You can also <a href="/djuma-birds/">explore recorded bird calls</a> while waiting.</p><noscript><p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@djumacam/live">Watch the stream on YouTube ↗</a></p></noscript></section>
<section class="section" id="naturecam"><div class="eyebrow">01 / The NatureCam project</div><div class="section-title"><h2>NatureCam at Djuma.</h2><p>NatureCam is Xeroth’s project to connect nature cameras, human expertise and AI. Djuma is where we’re starting—with the people who know this camera best.</p></div><div class="grid3"><article class="card"><span class="number">01 — OBSERVE</span><h3>A window open since 1998</h3><p>Djuma’s camera began sharing images in 1998. Generations of viewers and volunteer camera operators have built a shared record of life at the same waterhole—a history that gives each new observation context.</p><a class="link" href="https://www.youtube.com/@djumacam">Watch Djuma Cam ↗</a></article><article class="card"><span class="number">02 — RECOGNISE</span><h3>Hosana: who came back?</h3><p>Hosana is the re-identification pipeline we’re building. Its goal is to connect repeat sightings of the same animal over time, starting at Djuma. Individual recognition is still being researched and validated.</p><span class="tag">In development</span></article><article class="card"><span class="number">03 — ASSIST</span><h3>An AI companion for zoomies</h3><p>Zoomies are the people who remotely pan, tilt and zoom nature cameras. We’re developing an AI zoomie to help them notice activity, follow animals and choose useful views, with human judgement at the centre.</p><span class="tag">In development</span></article></div><div class="history"><div class="year">1998</div><p>Djuma’s first webcam began sending still images in August 1998. Later that year it moved to overlook Gowrie Dam. A community of viewers and remote camera operators grew around this enduring window into the South African bush. <a href="https://www.djuma.com/djumacam">Read Djuma’s history ↗</a></p></div></section>
''', 'NatureCam', extra_script='<script type="module" src="/assets/naturecam.js"></script>')
page('/acoustic-re-id-research/','Can AI be used to identify individual animals accurately?', 'Plain-English guide to our acoustic re-identification research, with the full paper, source code, dataset and model links.', f'''
<div class="subhero"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">OpenXeroth</a> / Acoustic re-identification</div><div class="eyebrow">Acoustic re-identification research</div><h1>Can AI be used to identify <br><em>individual animals accurately?</em></h1><p class="lead">Previous studies suggest AI can recognise individual animals from their calls. We found that recording conditions and the way recordings are divided for testing can inflate that apparent accuracy. Our study examines how much the background, microphone and recording session contribute to the result.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/papers/acoustic-re-id-v9.pdf">Read the full paper <span>↓ PDF</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="{repo}">Explore the code <span>↗</span></a></div></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><span class="big-number">9</span><small>species · eight birds and one bat</small></div><div class="stat"><span class="big-number">13</span><small>datasets evaluated</small></div><div class="stat"><span class="big-number">36</span><small>neural-network models, compared with two simple reference methods</small></div></div>
<section class="section prose"><div class="eyebrow">The question</div><h2>What could individual recognition make possible?</h2><p>Following the same animal over time could help researchers study survival, movement, social relationships and responses to environmental change. Recognising a familiar caller could extend that record beyond what a camera can see, with less need to catch, tag or disturb animals.</p><p>Earlier studies have approached this with machine learning (ML): computers learn patterns from labelled recordings and use them to suggest which animal made a new call. The promise is considerable, but a useful system must recognise an animal in a different session, with a different background, and when unfamiliar animals arrive.</p><p>Imagine recognising a friend on the phone. Their voice should remain familiar when they call from a busy street or a different handset. In animal recordings, a microphone or background can instead become an accidental name tag. We tested how much that affects the evidence for individual recognition.</p></section>
<figure class="research-visual"><div><span class="number">What goes into a recording?</span><div class="visual-components"><span>Animal’s voice</span><b>+</b><span>Background</span><b>+</b><span>Microphone &amp; session</span></div><div class="visual-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="visual-result">An AI identity prediction</div></div><figcaption>A good test asks which part of the recording supports the prediction. This is a conceptual diagram, not measured data.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="section"><div class="eyebrow">What we tested</div><h2>Four ways to check<br>if the computer got it right.</h2><article class="finding"><span class="num">01</span><div><h3>Change the recording session.</h3><p>The unit being divided matters. Training on some of Monday’s calls and testing on other calls from Monday can share background and recording conditions. Training on Monday and testing on Tuesday asks whether the model recognises the bird in a new session. We compared these designs using the same recordings and the same training and test counts for each bird. Across 18 comparisons, three species and ten fixed random splits, randomly dividing individual clips raised measured accuracy by 20–51 percentage points. That higher score does not show improved recognition on a new day. Whole sessions can themselves be assigned randomly while keeping their clips separate.</p></div></article><article class="finding"><span class="num">02</span><div><h3>Take the animal’s voice away.</h3><p>Could the system still predict the assigned identity from background alone? For chiffchaff recordings within a year, ordinary accuracy was 73.7% from backgrounds versus 76.5% from calls. Giving every bird equal weight produces balanced accuracies of 71.4% and 79.1%. The background result remains substantial, although the two scores are not equivalent. A high score can carry information about the recording context as well as the animal.</p></div></article><article class="finding"><span class="num">03</span><div><h3>Hold the microphone and recording constant.</h3><p>Where several animals shared a recording, we could ask whether the model distinguished them beyond that shared context. Of three datasets with this stronger test, only one zebra finch dataset gave clear evidence of identity information beyond the recording. Timing is also a limitation: the two BirdPark datasets contain no same-bird pairs from different recordings within seven days. They cannot provide a same-day check that separates recording changes from changes over time.</p></div></article><article class="finding"><span class="num">04</span><div><h3>Let strangers arrive.</h3><p>In nature, a caller may be an animal the system has never met. We set an acceptance threshold using separate unfamiliar animals, targeting no more than one in ten strangers. We then tested two ways of making a match: comparing a call with each familiar bird’s average sound profile, or with its closest individual training call. On wild datasets separated by session, the highest median proportion of familiar calls accepted and correctly named rose from 37% with average profiles to 46% with nearest-call matching. These are results for the tested methods, not limits on what future models can achieve. The median is over different assignments of birds to test roles, and measures calls rather than individual animals. Actual stranger acceptance could exceed the target; using more calibration strangers did not consistently solve this.</p></div></article><p class="fine">These are specific benchmark results, not forecasts of Djuma performance. See the paper for uncertainty intervals, dataset qualifications and the complete comparisons.</p></section>
<section class="section prose"><div class="eyebrow">The study at a glance</div><h2>Four checks, with their limits.</h2><figure><a href="/papers/graphical-abstract-v9.pdf"><img src="/assets/graphical-abstract-v9.png" alt="Study summary: random clip splits raise measured accuracy; background predicts assigned identity; timing limits shared-recording tests; open-set results depend on scoring and calibration." width="2000" height="1400" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto"></a><figcaption><a href="/papers/graphical-abstract-v9.pdf">Download the illustrated summary · PDF</a>. Read <a href="{repo}/tree/main/docs/review-sensitivity">the complete follow-up results and limitations</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>One further limitation remains: the added-background experiment used a single fixed assignment of background donors. Testing several donor assignments would be a separate robustness experiment.</p></section>
<section class="section"><div class="feature"><div><div class="eyebrow">Why it matters</div><h2>Better tests.<br>More trustworthy tools.</h2></div><div><p>We found no neural network that won on every dataset. Choosing a different internal layer also changed the rankings. The useful lesson is to test the entire recognition process against the conditions it will actually face.</p><p>For NatureCam and Hosana, that means treating individual identity as a claim to validate. Today’s BirdNET-Cloud detections at Djuma are species suggestions. They do not establish how many individual birds are present.</p><div class="notice">The study uses existing published datasets. It does not use Djuma Cam audio as a benchmark or demonstrate a working individual bird recogniser at Djuma.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="eyebrow">Read the paper. Inspect the methods.</div><h2>The work is here to explore.</h2><div class="paper-box"><span class="tag">Author manuscript · Version 9 · 5 October 2026</span><h3 style="margin-top:20px">Recording context and data splitting can inflate acoustic re-identification accuracy across species and studies</h3><p>Graham Wallington &amp; Jackie Lighten · Xeroth AI Limited</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/papers/acoustic-re-id-v9.pdf">Full paper · PDF ↓</a><a class="button secondary" href="/papers/supporting-information-v9.pdf">Supporting information · PDF ↓</a></div><p style="margin-top:22px">This author manuscript is prepared for final author approval before bioRxiv submission and has not been peer reviewed. The verification audit reproduced the reported error rates and checked all 224 original result files against their archive copies. The review follow-up adds 194 independently archived outputs covering matched splits, alternative scoring, calibration size and timing controls. Software and a separate evidence bundle are archived with permanent DOI links. Recordings, model weights and real identity annotations remain subject to their original publishers’ terms. <a href="{repo}/blob/main/docs/publication-status.md">Read the publication status</a>.</p><p>The paper and original evidence documentation use <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">CC BY-NC 4.0</a>: share and adapt with credit for non-commercial use. The software retains its Apache 2.0 licence. Xeroth AI Limited funded the research; both authors are company officers and shareholders.</p></div><div class="grid3" style="margin-top:22px"><article class="card"><h3>Source code</h3><p>Analysis methods, model adapters, controls and software tests.</p><a class="link" href="{repo}">Open the public repository ↗</a><br><a class="link" href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23167850">Archived software · DOI ↗</a></article><article class="card"><h3>Datasets &amp; models</h3><p>Original publishers, model registries and their separate licence terms.</p><a class="link" href="{repo}/blob/main/docs/datasets.md">Find the datasets ↗</a><br><a class="link" href="{repo}/blob/main/docs/models.md">Find the models ↗</a></article><article class="card"><h3>Reproducibility</h3><p>How to verify the software, source provenance and what a numerical rerun still requires.</p><a class="link" href="{repo}/blob/main/docs/reproduction.md">Read the reproduction guide ↗</a><br><a class="link" href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23167851">Evidence bundle · DOI ↗</a></article></div></section>''','Research')
for path,mode,title,lead in [('/djuma-birds/','feed','Who’s calling at Djuma?','A window into the birdsong around Gowrie Dam. Explore the latest species suggestions from Djuma Cam, in partnership with BirdNET-Cloud.'),('/djuma-birds/species/','species','A closer listen.','Explore this species’ detection history at Djuma Cam.'),('/djuma-birds/call/','call','One call. A moment in the bush.','An individual detection from Djuma Cam’s soundscape.')]:
    page(path,title,'Public BirdNET-Cloud species detections from Djuma Cam. Latest calls, species histories and individual detection records.',f'''<div id="birds-app" data-mode="{mode}"><div class="subhero"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">OpenXeroth</a> / <a href="/djuma-birds/">Djuma birds</a></div><div class="eyebrow">Djuma Cam × BirdNET-Cloud</div><h1 id="bird-title">{title}</h1><p class="lead" id="bird-lead">{lead}</p><div class="status" id="feed-status" role="status">Connecting to the detection feed…</div></div><div class="notice">These are automated species suggestions, not confirmed sightings or individual identities. Confidence is a model score, not the probability that a suggestion is correct. Times below are South African Standard Time (UTC+2).</div><div id="bird-content" aria-busy="true"><p class="loading">Loading the soundscape…</p></div></div><section class="section"><div class="prose"><h2>About these detections.</h2><p>One bird may produce many detections, and more than one bird may be present in a clip. Detection totals are not counts of animals. The feed refreshes every 60 seconds; upstream processing and interruptions can delay new records.</p><p><a href="https://birdnetcloud.com/s/djuma-cam-b1610b">Visit Djuma on BirdNET-Cloud ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@djumacam">Watch Djuma Cam ↗</a></p><a class="link" href="/acoustic-re-id-research/">Why recognising individuals is harder ↗</a></div></section>''','Djuma birds',True)
